{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>If a corpse is found in the land.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), the breaking of the heifer's neck in a wadi is a great stratagem so that the murderer be found, and this is the view of the Rambam, of blessed memory, and this is his language: because in most cases the murderer will be from the city nearest the corpse, and when the elders go out and engage in that measurement, the elders of the city testify before the Creator, blessed be He, that they were not negligent in maintaining the roads and guarding them and that they do not know who killed this man; and when the matter is investigated and the elders assemble and bring the heifer, many people will speak of it and the matter will become known. And they already said that even if a maidservant comes and says 'so-and-so is the murderer,' they do not break the neck. And if they know the murderer and keep silent, and testify before the Creator upon their lives that they did not know him — this would be a cause of great wantonness; and everyone who hears a rumor of the matter will come and tell, and the matter will be publicized, and he will be killed, whether by the court or the king or the blood-avenger. And the matter is reinforced by the fact that the place where the heifer's neck is broken is never to be worked or sown, and those who see it will recognize it and speak of it — end of the Rav's words, of blessed memory. And the expression וערפו means: they shall remove the neck of the heifer. And the reason for 'the city nearest the corpse' is that by reasonable assumption the murderer came from there, or they committed a transgression akin to it, for were it not so it would not have occurred that a person would be killed near them." "<b>And there are those</b> who explained 'a wadi' (נחל איתן) as a fertile and well-watered place, which shall not be worked or sown from this point onward; and so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded אשר לא יעבד בו ולא יזרע — for the future. And they said in the Mishnah: and its place is forbidden to sow but permitted to comb flax there or to pick out stones, for this is not labor upon the soil itself. And according to this interpretation, each and every person will be diligent in maintaining the roads so that no occasion for murder arises in any estate, for they know that they will lose their estate entirely and it will be forbidden for benefit forever in the soil itself." "<b>And by way of the Midrash</b> regarding the broken-necked heifer: the Holy One, blessed be He, said: let a heifer in its first year come and be broken in a place that produces no fruit, to atone for the killing of one who was not allowed to produce fruit. Those who expound harsh things said: therefore the Torah required a heifer that had never been worked, and a wadi that was never worked or sown, so that the heifer would not recognize it, and so that there would be no paved road there and it would not want to leave the wadi; and the priests would strike it on the back of its neck so that it would go out from the wadi — this is what is written: וערפו שם את העגלה בנחל. And at first the elders of the city would wash their hands over the heifer and say ידינו לא שפכו, and the priests would say כפר לעמך ישראל, and כפר in the sense of revealing, that the murderer be revealed to them. If Israel of that generation were meritorious, the heifer would go to the murderer's house and they would kill him there — this is ונכפר להם הדם, that is, the murderer is revealed to them. And if they were not meritorious and the heifer did not go, they would strike it with a hatchet at the back of its neck and break its neck there, and the priests would say כפר, in the sense of atonement, and then the Holy One, blessed be He, would grant atonement — this is ונכפר להם הדם." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel),</b> the matter of the broken-necked heifer in a wadi is that it is a place of destruction, like the matter of the two birds of the leper upon the face of the field, or the goat in the wilderness. For with the leper's two birds: the live bird, dipped in the blood of the slaughtered bird, was sent away upon the face of the field, among the birds of the field, and the live bird would bear his iniquity and he would be purified. And so the scapegoat bears upon itself the iniquities of Israel, as it is said (Leviticus 16:22): ונשא השעיר עליו את כל עונותם. And so too here, with the heifer broken in a wadi — a harsh place that was never worked or sown — the matter was for the prince of the wadi, and he bears upon it the sin of the murder. And it would have sufficed to say אשר לא יעבד ולא יזרע, but it said בו, to make clear that it must not be worked in its very substance, for by being unworked in its substance it is the place and portion of the prince of the wadi, and then: וערפו שם את העגלה בנחל — in that wadi which is his. And this is the meaning of איתן, for נחל איתן connotes that the wadi should be powerful, and also connotes the wadi of איתן, which is the prince appointed over it, who is a powerful and strong force. And it says ידינו לא שפכה with a ה, perhaps to hint that one who sheds human blood loses his five senses from him, and destroys the entire world that was created with the letter ה, for man was created unique and from him issued all subsequent generations. And it says כפר לעמך ישראל אשר פדית HaShem, for since the prosecuting force derived benefit from the breaking of this heifer, it is necessary to seek atonement from the Almighty, and with this they shall be atoned — this is what it says: ונכפר להם הדם. And so with the scapegoat, after it was sent to Azazel in the wilderness and the accuser received its portion, then the Holy One, blessed be He, would grant atonement, as it is written (ibid.): וכפר את מקדש הקדש וגו׳." "<b>Furthermore,</b> in the matter of the broken-necked heifer there is a great awakening to make known the nature of the corpse, when we see that Scripture commanded for the atonement of murder three things: the first, the breaking of the heifer's neck; the second, that the place be a wadi that shall not be worked or sown; and the third, a verbal confession, which is that the priests, the sons of Levi, should say: ידינו לא שפכה את הדם הזה כפר לעמך ישראל. And it is known to every understanding person that the murderer had dominion only by means of the separation of the bond, which separated from this corpse three souls: the animal soul, the vegetative soul, and the rational soul. And it is a known thing that the soul that departs from him first is the animal soul, and therefore He commanded a calf of the herd, which is the animal soul, corresponding to what was separated from him first, the animal soul. And the reason He commanded that the place be one that shall not be worked or sown — this corresponds to what was separated from him, the vegetative soul. And the reason He commanded a verbal confession, to say ידינו לא שפכו כפר לעמך ישראל — this corresponds to what was separated from him, the rational soul, which is the speaking soul. And the matter of separation makes known to the elite the nature of the corpse, and with this act ונכפר להם הדם. And I have heard that from the carcass of the heifer's body a worm is formed by way of nature, which goes and kills the murderer wherever he is; and this issues from the emanation of the prince of the wadi, and it is among the wonders of the hidden secrets of nature." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah.</b> The matter of the decapitated heifer (עגלה ערופה) — they would offer it to the attribute of harsh judgment, that it not be drawn against him and not rule over us. And the fact that it was from the neck (עורף) alludes to the matter of hardness, in the manner of (Deuteronomy 31:27) ״ואת ערפך הקשה״; and because HaShem, may He be exalted, turned toward us the neck and not the face when such a matter occurred in the Holy Land. And this is what it says, ״עגלת בקר״ — and it added בקר because its face is the face of an ox from the left side, and there sometimes the left becomes right and the right becomes left. ״לא עבד בה ולא משכה בעול״ — it did not serve with it and did not pull in a yoke, but rather it pulls a yoke upon others. And the word ״והורידו״ — that they should draw it down to נחל איתן; and ״וערפו שם״ — that they should make it weak there, from the language of (Genesis 49:24) ״משם רועה אבן ישראל״; the heifer in the נחל, to immerse it in waters called נחלי אל, and they are the attributes. And Scripture called the slain man חלל from the word חלול (hollowed), because he is hollowed of the soul that was within him; and further, from the word חולין, that the spirit of impurity profaned outside the city in the field — this is what it says, ״נופל בשדה״; and because this חלל already was and returned חלילה and rolled into him to light by the light of life which is upon the face of the ground, and this is what it says, ״חלל באדמה״ and it did not say בארץ. And this service was through the sons of Levi, for so is fitting, because by their word is every dispute and every plague, below and above; and the elders of the city wash their hands in the waters of חסד, which are above this heifer, so that the attribute of judgment not rule over them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :register {:flags {3 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:43.957015126Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}